  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071504/assets/fd02b87f9c6c1676617eb9ad8837e53a73d69e936beb5571e91f4760e44dccc5.png)\n\nLet $U={1,2,3,4,a,b,c}$ be the universal set and let $V={1,b}.$ What is $\\overset{\u2015}{V}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The complement of $V,$ denoted $\\overset{\u2015}{V},$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $V.$ To represent $\\overset{\u2015}{V},$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $V,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071504/assets/5997d1aa469784f2bdddf0f90c6ca7f8c9dc0ff83886be35b28306ac1246a08b.png)\n\nThe elements in the shaded area are $2,3,4,a,$ and $c.$ These are the elements of $U$ that are not in $V.$ Therefore, the complement of $V$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{\u2015}{V}={2,3,4,a,c}.\n$$",
